###Pure
// ============================================================
// collateral-opt -- collateral optimisation against index eligibility
// ============================================================
// collateral-core answers "is this piece allowed to be posted here". It does not answer the
// question the collateral desk actually asks every morning, which is "of everything in the
// box, WHICH piece should go to WHICH agreement". That is this project.
//
// Three costs decide it, and a real optimiser cannot drop any of them:
//
//   HAIRCUT      posting 100 of a 15%-haircut asset buys 85 of collateral value, so the
//                expensive asset ties up more of the balance sheet for the same call.
//   FUNDING      the asset had to be funded to be in the box at all.
//   OPPORTUNITY  the asset could have been LENT instead. A special that borrows at 300bp is
//                the most expensive thing on the shelf to pledge, however cheap its haircut,
//                and an optimiser that ignores the lending desk posts exactly the wrong bond.
//
// Cheapest-to-deliver is the minimum of that stack over the pieces that pass the agreement's
// Schedule A, subject to the concentration caps -- which is why the plan has to be rolled up
// per issuer before it can be called feasible.
//
// Layer 2. Depends on collateral-core (agreements, Schedule A, the pieces already posted) and
// index-core (constituents, weights, the rebalance). Nothing else.
//
// WHY INDEX MEMBERSHIP IS HERE AND NOT DECORATION: an index constituent has a published
// weight, a daily struck level and a queue of tracking funds obliged to hold it. That makes it
// cheap to value, cheap to substitute and hard to dispute -- so a constituent earns a haircut
// relief and a lower dispute risk, and an agreement's schedule can be negotiated as "anything
// in this index above this weight" instead of as a list of names that goes stale at every
// rebalance. CopIndexBasketRule is that clause.
//
// TWO OR-JOINS ARE IN PLAY AND THEY ARE DIFFERENT DISJUNCTIONS:
//
//   collateral-core's   issuer OR asset class  -- is this piece ELIGIBLE
//   this project's      instrument OR issuer   -- is this piece IN DEMAND to be lent
//
// The second is `CopLendingOpportunity` below. Securities-lending demand is quoted either
// against a specific line ("the 2031 OAT") or against a whole issuer as general collateral,
// never both on one quote, and a lot earns the fee if it matches on either axis. Both ends
// are to-MANY, for the same reason collateral-core's are: an unqualified `or` returns every
// quote that hits, and the opportunity cost is the HIGHEST of them, not an arbitrary one.
//
// Package root collateral_opt::, prefixes COP_ / Cop_ / Cop / cop per the contract.

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ what the optimiser is told

// FOR: one execution of the optimiser -- a dated, re-runnable decision that can be explained
// after the fact. Everything below hangs off a run because "why did we post that" is a
// question asked weeks later, and the answer is only reconstructible if the inputs, the
// scores and the plan are all stamped with the same run.
Class collateral_opt::CopOptimisationRun
{
   runId: String[1];
   runDate: StrictDate[1];
   // MIN_COST, MIN_OPPORTUNITY_COST, MAX_INDEX_QUALITY -- what was being minimised, because
   // the same box produces different plans under different objectives and the objective is
   // the first thing anyone disputes.
   objective: String[1];
   // OPTIMAL, FEASIBLE, INFEASIBLE, TIMED_OUT. A plan from a TIMED_OUT run is a suggestion.
   solverStatus: String[1];
   costBasisId: String[0..1];
   totalPostedValue: Float[1];
   totalOpportunityCost: Float[1];
   iterationCount: Integer[0..1];
   // Committed means the plan was released to operations; an uncommitted run is a what-if.
   isCommitted: Boolean[1];

   // What the plan is worth once the lending revenue it gives up is charged against it. The
   // number that decides whether optimising was worth doing at all.
   netBenefit() { $this.totalPostedValue - $this.totalOpportunityCost } : Float[1];
}

// FOR: the price list the optimiser reasons with -- funding spread, substitution cost, the
// weight put on haircut, and the relief an index constituent earns.
//
// Separate from the run because it is negotiated with treasury once a quarter and then used by
// every run: a plan that changed because the cost basis changed is a different fact from a
// plan that changed because the box changed.
Class collateral_opt::CopCostBasis
{
   costBasisId: String[1];
   name: String[1];
   // What the firm pays to hold the asset it is about to pledge.
   fundingSpreadBps: Float[1];
   // The all-in cost of pulling one piece back and putting another in -- settlement, fails
   // risk, and the counterparty's right to refuse. Substitution is never free.
   substitutionCostBps: Float[1];
   // How hard the objective leans on haircut versus opportunity cost.
   haircutPenaltyWeight: Float[1];
   // The relief an index constituent earns for being easy to value and to replace.
   indexMemberReliefBps: Float[1];
   valuationCurrency: String[1];
   effectiveDate: StrictDate[1];

   // The spread restated as a fraction, which is the form every cost line actually multiplies
   // by. Float because `/` widens.
   fundingCostFraction() { $this.fundingSpreadBps / 10000.0 } : Float[1];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ what there is to post

// FOR: one lot in the box that COULD be posted -- the optimiser's supply side.
//
// Deliberately NOT collateral_core::ColCollateral: that class is a piece already pledged under
// an agreement, and the whole point of an optimiser is to reason about assets before they are
// pledged. A lot becomes a ColCollateral only if a plan line is executed.
//
// `instrumentId` and `issuerId` are OWN columns because they are the left-hand side of this
// project's or-join, exactly as collateral-core denormalises its two axes onto COL_COLLATERAL:
// a join condition cannot hop through the instrument master to reach an issuer.
Class collateral_opt::CopInventoryLot
{
   lotId: String[1];
   // Axis one of the lending disjunction.
   instrumentId: String[0..1];
   // Axis two of the lending disjunction.
   issuerId: String[0..1];
   assetClass: String[1];
   custodyAccountId: String[0..1];
   quantity: Float[1];
   marketValue: Float[1];
   currency: String[1];
   ratingBand: String[0..1];
   residualYears: Float[0..1];
   // Already pledged, lent or otherwise spoken for. Encumbered stock is in the box and is not
   // available, and conflating the two is how an optimiser produces an infeasible plan.
   isEncumbered: Boolean[1];
   availableFrom: StrictDate[1];
   withdrawnDate: StrictDate[0..1];

   // Still in the box. A lot leaves by a date, never by deleting the row.
   isAvailable() { $this.withdrawnDate->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   // Market value per unit -- Float, because `/` widens even between two Integers.
   unitValue() { $this.marketValue / $this.quantity } : Float[1];
}

// FOR: a securities-lending quote against this stock -- the OPPORTUNITY COST of pledging it.
//
// THE RIGHT-HAND SIDE OF THIS PROJECT'S OR-JOIN. A quote is written against a named line or
// against an issuer as general collateral, never both on one row, so exactly one of
// `instrumentId` / `issuerId` is populated and `demandBasis` records which. A lot earns the
// fee if it matches on either axis, which is an `or` in the join and not two joins.
Class collateral_opt::CopLendingDemand
{
   demandId: String[1];
   // INSTRUMENT or ISSUER -- which of the two columns below is the live one.
   demandBasis: String[1];
   instrumentId: String[0..1];
   issuerId: String[0..1];
   // What a borrower pays. This is the number that is forgone by posting the asset instead.
   borrowFeeBps: Float[1];
   demandQuantity: Float[0..1];
   demandCurrency: String[1];
   quoteDate: StrictDate[1];
   expiryDate: StrictDate[0..1];
   // A special: hard to borrow, expensive, and the last thing that should ever be pledged.
   isSpecial: Boolean[1];

   // The two halves of the disjunction, asked of a single quote.
   isInstrumentSpecific() { $this.instrumentId->isNotEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   isGeneralCollateral() { $this.instrumentId->isEmpty() && $this.issuerId->isNotEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
}

// FOR: a Schedule A clause written as INDEX MEMBERSHIP rather than as a list of names --
// "any constituent of this index carrying at least 0.05% weight".
//
// It hangs off collateral_core::ColEligibilitySchedule, so it extends an existing negotiated
// schedule instead of replacing it, and it points at index_core::IdxIndex, so the eligible
// universe is restruck by the index provider at every rebalance and never goes stale in the
// contract. The weight floor is the liquidity proxy; the ceiling stops the clause admitting
// the one mega-cap the counterparty is already long.
Class collateral_opt::CopIndexBasketRule
{
   basketRuleId: String[1];
   scheduleId: String[1];
   indexId: String[1];
   // The liquidity floor: below this weight a constituent is not reliably replaceable.
   minIndexWeightPct: Float[1];
   maxIndexWeightPct: Float[1];
   // Whether membership is required or merely rewarded.
   requiresConstituent: Boolean[1];
   // The haircut given back for being index-quality.
   haircutReliefPct: Float[1];
   effectiveDate: StrictDate[1];
   withdrawnDate: StrictDate[0..1];

   // How wide the admitted band actually is; a clause with a narrow window admits almost
   // nothing and is usually a negotiation that went wrong.
   weightWindowPct() { $this.maxIndexWeightPct - $this.minIndexWeightPct } : Float[1];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ the decision

// FOR: the RESOLVED eligibility verdict for one lot against one agreement, on one run.
//
// This class exists because collateral-core's or-join is to-many on both ends: a French
// government bond matches the "French Republic" line AND the "G10 govvies" line, and the
// answer is the tightest of them with any exclusion applied afterwards. Nothing downstream
// should re-derive that. `matchedRuleCount` keeps the evidence, `bindingRule` names the line
// that actually bound, and `tightestHaircutPct` is what the optimiser costed with.
//
// Composite grain (run, lot, agreement): the same lot is eligible under one CSA and not
// another, and the verdict is only true as of the run that computed it.
Class collateral_opt::CopEligibilityAssessment
{
   runId: String[1];
   lotId: String[1];
   agreementId: String[1];
   // Which Schedule A line bound, out of the many the disjunction returned.
   bindingRuleId: String[0..1];
   // How many rules the or-join matched. Greater than one is normal, not an error.
   matchedRuleCount: Integer[1];
   tightestHaircutPct: Float[1];
   isEligible: Boolean[1];
   // An exclusion rule carves out what a broader line let in; the or-join returns it like any
   // other match, so it has to be applied AFTER the join and recorded here.
   isExcluded: Boolean[1];
   exclusionReason: String[0..1];
   // Whether an index basket clause is what made this lot eligible.
   admittedByIndexBasket: Boolean[1];

   // More than one Schedule A line hit this lot, so a human reading `bindingRule` alone is
   // seeing an answer and not the whole match set.
   hasCompetingRules() { $this.matchedRuleCount > 1 } : Boolean[1];
   // Eligible in the end: matched something and was not carved back out.
   isPostable() { $this.isEligible && $this.isExcluded->not() } : Boolean[1];
}

// FOR: the cost stack that makes one lot cheaper to deliver than another, per agreement.
//
// Kept apart from the verdict above because eligibility is a contractual fact and this is an
// economic opinion: the same lot scores differently under a different cost basis without any
// contract having changed. The components are stored, not just the total -- an optimiser
// nobody can decompose is an optimiser nobody follows.
Class collateral_opt::CopDeliveryScore
{
   runId: String[1];
   lotId: String[1];
   agreementId: String[1];
   // Cost of the collateral value lost to the haircut.
   haircutCostBps: Float[1];
   // Cost of having funded the asset in the first place.
   fundingCostBps: Float[1];
   // The lending revenue given up by pledging it. The term optimisers most often omit.
   opportunityCostBps: Float[1];
   // The relief earned by index membership, subtracted rather than added.
   indexReliefBps: Float[1];
   totalCostBps: Float[1];
   // 1 is cheapest-to-deliver for this agreement on this run.
   rankInAgreement: Integer[0..1];

   // The stored total restated from its parts, so a stale or hand-edited total is visible.
   recomputedTotalBps() { $this.haircutCostBps + $this.fundingCostBps + $this.opportunityCostBps - $this.indexReliefBps } : Float[1];
}

// FOR: what the run decided to post to ONE agreement -- the requirement, what was found, and
// whether the box could actually cover it.
Class collateral_opt::CopPostingPlan
{
   planId: String[1];
   runId: String[1];
   agreementId: String[1];
   // The call to be met, net of thresholds and MTA.
   requiredValue: Float[1];
   // The post-haircut collateral value the plan's lines add up to.
   plannedValue: Float[1];
   currency: String[1];
   shortfallValue: Float[1];
   // False when the eligible, unencumbered box cannot cover the call -- the answer the desk
   // needs earliest in the day.
   isFeasible: Boolean[1];
   planStatus: String[1];

   // Covered on the plan's own numbers.
   isCovered() { $this.plannedValue >= $this.requiredValue } : Boolean[1];
   // How much of the requirement the plan meets. Float, because `/` widens.
   coveragePct() { ($this.plannedValue * 100.0) / $this.requiredValue } : Float[1];
}

// FOR: one lot proposed against one plan -- the grain of the whole project, and the rows both
// aggregations below roll up.
//
// It carries `runId`, `agreementId` and `issuerId` as its own columns even though they are
// reachable through the plan and the lot: a `~groupBy` view groups on COLUMNS of the table it
// reads, so the grouping keys have to be present here or the rollups cannot be written.
Class collateral_opt::CopPostingPlanLine
{
   lineId: String[1];
   planId: String[1];
   runId: String[1];
   agreementId: String[1];
   lotId: String[1];
   // Denormalised for the concentration rollup, and for nothing else.
   issuerId: String[0..1];
   assetClass: String[1];
   quantity: Float[1];
   marketValue: Float[1];
   appliedHaircutPct: Float[1];
   // Market value after the haircut -- what the counterparty actually credits.
   postValue: Float[1];
   // The lending fee forgone on this line.
   opportunityCost: Float[1];
   lineRank: Integer[0..1];
   // True when this line displaces something already posted rather than filling a new call.
   isSubstitution: Boolean[1];

   // The haircut restated as money.
   haircutAmount() { $this.marketValue - $this.postValue } : Float[1];
   // What the line is worth once the lending revenue it costs is charged against it.
   netPostValue() { $this.postValue - $this.opportunityCost } : Float[1];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ the aggregations

// FOR: the plan totalled per (run, agreement) -- the AGGREGATION over the store's `~groupBy`
// view IDX-style, and the only row an operations screen needs.
//
// A plan with four hundred lines becomes one row: how much is being posted, what it costs in
// forgone lending, and how many lines have to settle. The engine folds the GROUP BY into the
// SQL, so nothing here is materialised and nothing seeds it.
//
// A grouping produces no row for an empty group: an agreement the run posted nothing to is
// ABSENT here rather than present at zero, which is exactly the case a feasibility report must
// not read off this class.
Class collateral_opt::CopPlanAgreementSummary
{
   runId: String[1];
   agreementId: String[1];
   totalPostValue: Float[1];
   totalOpportunityCost: Float[1];
   lineCount: Integer[1];

   // The plan's economics in one number.
   netOfOpportunity() { $this.totalPostValue - $this.totalOpportunityCost } : Float[1];
}

// FOR: the same plan rolled up one axis finer -- per (run, agreement, issuer) -- because a
// concentration limit is breached by the PORTFOLIO and not by a piece.
//
// This is the aggregation that makes the optimiser correct rather than merely cheap. Every
// line in a plan can be individually eligible and the plan can still put 40% of the posted
// value with one issuer, and the only place that is visible is a rollup. It joins straight
// onto collateral_core::ColConcentrationLimit on the issuer axis, so the cap and the number it
// caps sit side by side.
Class collateral_opt::CopIssuerConcentration
{
   runId: String[1];
   agreementId: String[1];
   issuerId: String[1];
   postedValue: Float[1];
   lotCount: Integer[1];

   // Average size of the lots going to this issuer. `->toFloat()` because Integer against
   // Float types as Number, and Number is not a Float.
   averageLotValue() { $this.postedValue / $this.lotCount->toFloat() } : Float[1];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ acting on the answer

// FOR: a swap the optimiser wants to make -- pull back something expensive that is already
// posted, and put a cheaper lot in its place.
//
// The outgoing side is a collateral_core::ColCollateral, because it is already pledged; the
// incoming side is a CopInventoryLot, because it is not. That asymmetry is the whole class:
// a substitution is the only way an optimiser improves a position it did not create.
Class collateral_opt::CopSubstitutionProposal
{
   proposalId: String[1];
   runId: String[1];
   agreementId: String[1];
   outgoingCollateralId: String[1];
   incomingLotId: String[0..1];
   valueDifference: Float[1];
   // The saving net of the substitution cost -- a swap that saves less than it costs to do is
   // a proposal that should never be raised.
   costSavingBps: Float[1];
   proposalStatus: String[1];
   raisedDate: StrictDate[1];
   withdrawnDate: StrictDate[0..1];

   isOpen() { $this.withdrawnDate->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
   // Worth doing only once the counterparty's right to refuse and the settlement cost are
   // priced in; the raw saving is not the test.
   isWorthDoing() { $this.costSavingBps > 0.0 && $this.withdrawnDate->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ THE OR-JOIN

// THE DISJUNCTION THIS PROJECT ADDS, and it is a different one from collateral-core's.
//
// A lot is in lending demand if its INSTRUMENT is quoted, or if its ISSUER is quoted as
// general collateral. Both ends to-MANY, and not out of laziness: one GC quote against an
// issuer hits every line that issuer has in the box, and one bond is hit by both the specific
// quote and the GC quote. The opportunity cost is the MAXIMUM over the returned set --
// `$lot.lendingDemands.borrowFeeBps->max()` -- and declaring either end [0..1] would compile
// happily and then cost the desk the difference.
Association collateral_opt::CopLendingOpportunity
{
   lendingDemands: collateral_opt::CopLendingDemand[*];
   demandedLots: collateral_opt::CopInventoryLot[*];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ associations, internal

// The price list a run reasoned with. [0..1] because a what-if run can be executed against
// defaults before a basis is agreed.
Association collateral_opt::CopRunCostBasis
{
   costBasis: collateral_opt::CopCostBasis[0..1];
   runs: collateral_opt::CopOptimisationRun[*];
}

Association collateral_opt::CopRunPlans
{
   run: collateral_opt::CopOptimisationRun[1];
   plans: collateral_opt::CopPostingPlan[*];
}

Association collateral_opt::CopPlanLines
{
   plan: collateral_opt::CopPostingPlan[1];
   lines: collateral_opt::CopPostingPlanLine[*];
}

Association collateral_opt::CopLotPlanLines
{
   lot: collateral_opt::CopInventoryLot[1];
   plannedLines: collateral_opt::CopPostingPlanLine[*];
}

Association collateral_opt::CopRunAssessments
{
   assessmentRun: collateral_opt::CopOptimisationRun[1];
   assessments: collateral_opt::CopEligibilityAssessment[*];
}

// To-many on the assessment end: one lot is assessed against every agreement in scope.
Association collateral_opt::CopLotAssessments
{
   assessedLot: collateral_opt::CopInventoryLot[1];
   lotAssessments: collateral_opt::CopEligibilityAssessment[*];
}

Association collateral_opt::CopRunScores
{
   scoreRun: collateral_opt::CopOptimisationRun[1];
   deliveryScores: collateral_opt::CopDeliveryScore[*];
}

Association collateral_opt::CopLotScores
{
   scoredLot: collateral_opt::CopInventoryLot[1];
   lotScores: collateral_opt::CopDeliveryScore[*];
}

// The rollup reached from the plan it rolls up. [0..1] both ways: the view is keyed on
// exactly the pair the plan is keyed on, and the row is absent when the plan has no lines.
Association collateral_opt::CopPlanSummaryLink
{
   planSummary: collateral_opt::CopPlanAgreementSummary[0..1];
   summarisedPlan: collateral_opt::CopPostingPlan[0..1];
}

// The finer rollup: one row per issuer the plan touches.
Association collateral_opt::CopPlanConcentrations
{
   concentrationPlan: collateral_opt::CopPostingPlan[0..1];
   issuerConcentrations: collateral_opt::CopIssuerConcentration[*];
}

Association collateral_opt::CopRunSubstitutions
{
   substitutionRun: collateral_opt::CopOptimisationRun[1];
   substitutionProposals: collateral_opt::CopSubstitutionProposal[*];
}

Association collateral_opt::CopProposalIncomingLot
{
   incomingLot: collateral_opt::CopInventoryLot[0..1];
   incomingProposals: collateral_opt::CopSubstitutionProposal[*];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ crossing into collateral-core

// The agreement the plan posts to. [0..1] on the agreement end: a run can be scoped to an
// agreement that has since been terminated, and the plan is still the record of what was
// decided.
Association collateral_opt::CopPlanAgreement
{
   agreement: collateral_core::ColCollateralAgreement[0..1];
   postingPlans: collateral_opt::CopPostingPlan[*];
}

// Which ONE of the many rules the or-join returned actually bound. The resolution of
// collateral-core's to-many match, made navigable.
Association collateral_opt::CopAssessmentBindingRule
{
   bindingRule: collateral_core::ColEligibilityRule[0..1];
   boundAssessments: collateral_opt::CopEligibilityAssessment[*];
}

// The index clause attaches to the negotiated schedule, extending Schedule A rather than
// replacing it.
Association collateral_opt::CopBasketRuleSchedule
{
   basketSchedule: collateral_core::ColEligibilitySchedule[0..1];
   indexBasketRules: collateral_opt::CopIndexBasketRule[*];
}

// The cap beside the number it caps. To-many both ways: a limit scoped to an issuer bites on
// every run's rollup for that issuer, and one rollup row can face a limit from more than one
// schedule.
Association collateral_opt::CopConcentrationAgainstLimit
{
   bindingLimits: collateral_core::ColConcentrationLimit[*];
   issuerRollups: collateral_opt::CopIssuerConcentration[*];
}

// The piece being pulled back. Already pledged, so it is a ColCollateral and not a lot.
Association collateral_opt::CopProposalOutgoing
{
   outgoingCollateral: collateral_core::ColCollateral[0..1];
   optimiserProposals: collateral_opt::CopSubstitutionProposal[*];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ crossing into index-core

// Why index membership is worth a haircut relief, made navigable: from a lot to every
// constituent row its instrument has. To-many both ways -- one instrument is a constituent of
// several indices and of the same index at every rebalance, and one constituent line covers
// every lot of that instrument in the box.
//
// From here index-core's own chain does the rest:
//
//   $lot.indexMemberships.rebalance.regionWeights
//   $lot.indexMemberships.issuerCountry.subRegion.macroRegion.code
//
// and this project declares no join for either.
Association collateral_opt::CopLotIndexMembership
{
   indexMemberships: index_core::IdxConstituent[*];
   inventoryLots: collateral_opt::CopInventoryLot[*];
}

// The index a basket clause is written against. The eligible universe is restruck by the
// provider at every rebalance, which is the entire reason to write the clause this way.
Association collateral_opt::CopBasketRuleIndex
{
   basketIndex: index_core::IdxIndex[0..1];
   collateralBasketRules: collateral_opt::CopIndexBasketRule[*];
}
